  "description": "#3826 — the PAGINATED half of the read differential: three consecutive `list_blocks` pages over one parent's five children, each page after the first driven by the `next_cursor` the previous page returned (`cursor_from`). A single-page fixture would pin nothing about pagination: per #3821, a keyset-ordering divergence changes WHICH rows a page contains, not merely their order, so the boundary only becomes visible once a second page is fetched with the first page's cursor. `has_more` flips false on the last page, and the pages are `ordered` because `list_children` keysets on `(position, id)` over seed blocks whose ids are byte-identical on both stacks.",
